[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: ispa, ispb
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Edge/ISP routing policy. The default routes on the simulated ISP routers point back into the customer, creating a routing loop for all non-local traffic.

Mechanism: PC1 (10.1.0.100) reaches its gateway 10.1.0.1 and other internal hosts fine. The campus core learns a default from r1 and forwards internet-bound traffic to r1. r1’s own default route is via ispa (203.0.113.1). However, ispa’s default route points back to r1 (203.0.113.2), and ispb’s default also points back to r1 (203.0.113.6). Packets to external destinations bounce between r1 and the ISPs until TTL expires, so web requests “spin” and time out.

Localization: 
- r1: default via 203.0.113.1 (ispa) [r1 ip_route].
- ispa: default via 203.0.113.2 (r1) [ispa ip_route].
- ispb: default via 203.0.113.6 (r1) [ispb ip_route].
- Ping to 1.1.1.1 from ispb returns “TTL exceeded” from 192.0.2.1 (ispa) confirming the loop. PC1 also cannot reach 1.1.1.1.

Root cause: Misconfigured static default routes on ispa and ispb pointing to the customer router r1, causing a routing loop for internet-bound traffic.

Corrective fix: On ispa and ispb, remove/replace the default routes that point to r1. Point their defaults to a proper upstream (or, in this lab, to the intended “internet” next hop) and advertise a usable default toward r1. Ensure only the ISP side injects default toward the campus, and never uses the customer as its own default. After correction, verify: r1 can ping 1.1.1.1, and PC1 can load external sites. === END ===